Junior In-Situ Recovery Operators
Ur-Energy Inc. (URG) and enCore Energy Corp (EU) offer equity ownership in operational and ramping ISR wellfields in Wyoming and Texas. Investors own low-capital-intensity extraction facilities, such as Ur-Energy’s Lost Creek plant and enCore’s Rosita and Alta Mesa facilities. These operating assets benefit from low initial capital expenditure requirements compared to conventional underground mines, but remain exposed to localized wellfield ramp-up delays, scale constraints, and cash flow volatility during early-stage operational scaling.   

Royalty and Streaming Companies
Uranium Royalty Corp (UROY) provides contractual top-line gross revenues, net smelter returns (NSR), and physical commodity holdings across third-party extraction projects. Shareholders own direct top-line revenue claims without exposure to mine-level operating costs, sustaining capital expenditure, or direct environmental reclamation liabilities. However, long-term portfolio growth depends on corporate capital deployment to acquire new royalty streams, which is frequently financed through equity issuances.   

Enrichment and Downstream Fuel Services
Centrus Energy Corp (LEU) provides exposure to the downstream enrichment segment of the nuclear fuel cycle rather than upstream extraction. Shareholders own a specialized service business supplying Low-Enriched Uranium (LEU) and developing High-Assay Low-Enriched Uranium (HALEU) centrifuge cascade capacity under federal contracts with the U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) and commercial utility customers. The vehicle is insulated from mining cash cost inflation but is exposed to federal budget appropriations, geopolitical trade policies, and technology deployment timelines.   

